  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 141 PM MDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe 594 to 596 dam 500 mb upper high flattens over much of the \ndesert SW on Monday. Generally drier air remains in place with PWATS \naround 0.6 to 0.9 inches. This combined with downslope surface \nsouthwest and west winds, will allow the heat to reintensify across \ncentral and eastern NM. Record highs in the upper 90s to mid 100s \nwill be common across lower elevations. Heat Advisories will likely \nbe needed again across the Northwest and West Central Plateau and \nexpanded to numerous central and eastern zones come Monday. A few \nExtreme Heat Warnings cannot be ruled out as well, including \nFarmington and Albuquerque. Isolated to scattered storms Monday \nafternoon and evening will mainly be confined to the higher terrain \nand northeast highlands and far northeast plains. \n\nA disturbance moving across eastern CO Monday evening will send a \nboundary through northeast NM Tuesday morning cooling down high \ntemperatures by 5 to 10  degrees across this part of the state. \nAdditionally, PWATS across most of the area increase to around 0.8 \nto 1.2 inches. This will result in a slightly higher coverage of \nshowers and storms across the high terrain and nearby lower \nelevations. For Wednesday into next weekend, to upper high over the \ndesert SW looks to reintensify to the upper 590s dam at 500 mb and \npivot to more over NV, UT, and CO. High temperatures will be hotter \nthan average for early August with mid 90s to low 100s across lower \nelevations. Diurnal shower and thunderstorm chances favoring the \nnorth central and southwest mountains and nearby lower elevations \nmoving south-southeast in nearby lower elevations across central and \neastern NM and west-southwest into nearby lower elevations across \nwestern NM.",
